\paragraph{Framework applicability.}
The diagnostic framework applies most directly to environments with two properties: sparse terminal rewards (which cause step-level oracle degeneracy) and catastrophic failure modes (which generate asymmetric death signal). ScienceWorld satisfies both; MATH satisfies neither (dense per-problem reward, no death), and our framework correctly identifies the absence of death-driven signal there. Environments with graded failure costs (partial penalties rather than binary death) would yield intermediate DAF values. As model capabilities improve, we predict DAF will decrease: stronger backbones with lower hallucination rates will die less often, narrowing the death-rate differential $\delta$ and potentially revealing non-death routing signal currently masked by catastrophic failures.

\paragraph{Modality heterogeneity.}
The deliberate heterogeneity in deterministic modality definition---ground-truth state observation in ScienceWorld, code execution in MATH, admissible-action filtering in ALFWorld---tests whether the framework generalizes, not whether any single modality definition does. The consistent finding across structurally different modalities strengthens rather than weakens the conclusion that catastrophic-failure avoidance is the dominant routing signal wherever it exists.

